<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oxford is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, home to one of the oldest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was among the universities where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> was studied. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> note that Justinian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Oxford, became a foundation of European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford.jpg" alt="Oxford" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tony Hisgett from Birmingham, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_of_Oxford_(5652685943).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
